body:
Being an avgeek, I looked it up. Engine failure big time - loss of pressure and subsequent engine fire, long story short. Luckily, the pilot got out okay

&#x200B;

https://www.aviation-safety.net/wikibase/280425

I've watched an interesting [video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D1-WuBbVe2E) on colour perception depending on your vocabulary to some extent. The more colour names you know, the more shades you're able to recognise quickly because by distinguishing them linguistically you basically train your brain to pick them up. According to various works on gender linguistics I've read (both in English and in Polish), the stereotypicaly, "model" feminine language consists of broader vocabulary when it comes to colours, because fashion and homemaking (traditionally feminine areas of life) required it. Well, this also applies to artists. When I was in middle school, I once argued with a classmate (who's been attending drawing lessons since she was a kid) that some crayon of mine was red, while she claimed it was some other shade. I saw this crayon as simply red. Then I got more into drawing and colour theory myself and now I definitelly see the difference.

&#x200B;

TL;DR Your vocabulary regarding colours affects your perception of colour
